iommu/omap: do not sleep while holding the iommu spinlocks

Starting the IPU produces two "BUG: scheduling while atomic" splats on
every single start, reproducibly, with identical preempt counts:

  BUG: scheduling while atomic: pyra-m4-run.sh/6110/0x00000003
   usleep_range           <- sleeps
   omap_reset_deassert
   sysc_runtime_resume
   __rpm_callback / rpm_callback / rpm_resume / rpm_resume
   __pm_runtime_resume
   omap_iommu_attach_dev  <- holds two spinlocks
   __iommu_attach_group
   iommu_attach_device
   rproc_boot
   state_store

omap_iommu_attach_dev() takes spin_lock(&omap_domain->lock), then
omap_iommu_attach() takes spin_lock(&obj->iommu_lock), and inside both
of them iommu_enable() calls pm_runtime_get_sync().  On this SoC that
resumes the mmu's ti-sysc target module, which deasserts its hardreset
line and polls RSTST with readl_relaxed_poll_timeout(..., 1, ...) --
that is a usleep_range, so the kernel sleeps with two spinlocks held.
dma_map_single() is inside the same region for the same reason.

The second splat is a consequence of the first, not a separate bug.
schedule_debug() responds to an atomic schedule by forcing the count
with preempt_count_set(PREEMPT_DISABLED), i.e. to 1; the two
spin_unlock()s that follow then take it 1 -> 0 -> -1, which is the
0xffffffff in the second report.  From there every sleep in the rest of
that syscall -- in our case the CMA allocation in rproc_alloc_carveout()
-- reports atomic too.

None of this is specific to 5.6 or to the backport in 0009.  Mainline
today is character-for-character identical here: omap_iommu_domain still
declares spinlock_t lock, omap_iommu still declares spinlock_t
iommu_lock, and iommu_enable() still calls pm_runtime_get_sync() between
them.  What the Pyra's config changes is only whether anyone is told:
CONFIG_PREEMPT=y and CONFIG_SMP=y, so preempt_count is maintained and
schedule_debug() notices.  A PREEMPT_NONE vendor kernel sleeps under the
same held locks in the same place and says nothing, which is presumably
why this has survived so long -- an OMAP5 or DRA7 that never attaches an
iommu domain to the IPU or DSP never reaches the code at all, and 0010
and 0011 are what make it reachable here.

Both locks can simply be mutexes.  Every site that takes either one is
already a sleeping path:

  obj->iommu_lock    omap_iommu_attach(), omap_iommu_detach()
  omap_domain->lock  omap_iommu_attach_dev(), omap_iommu_detach_dev()

and that is the complete list -- neither is touched by
iommu_fault_handler() or by any other interrupt context, and the map and
unmap paths do not take them at all.  So this does not move work out
from under a lock or change what is serialised; it only stops the sleep
being illegal.

linux/mutex.h is added to omap-iommu.h because the struct definitions
there now need the complete type; omap-iommu.c already included it.

Built and booted on the V5.3 unit on 2026-09-20.  Both splats are gone,
across two start/stop cycles, and the only WARNING left in the log is the
omap_l3_noc one this machine prints at t=2.5s on every kernel including
stock.  device/pyra-m4-run.sh goes from 10 ok / 1 failed to 11 ok / 0
failed.  Starting the IPU also got quicker -- 8 ms from "powering up" to
"is now up", against about 108 ms before, because the boot no longer goes
through __schedule_bug twice.

The remaining risk is entirely in whether some caller of
iommu_attach_device()/iommu_detach_device() somewhere else in the tree is
in atomic context, which would previously have been legal and is not any
more.  Nothing on this machine is: the only iommu domains here belong to
the ipu and the dsp.

